  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Her blood knocked out 23 hospital workers in minutes. In February 1994, Gloria Ramirez was rushed into a California emergency room with severe heart complications. But as nurses drew her blood, the room suddenly smelled like ammonia and garlic.",
        "visual_keywords": "hospital hallway flashing lights"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Inside the syringe, strange white crystals floated in her dark blood. Seconds later, the nurse holding the needle collapsed. Another passed out cold, and a third lost control of her limbs as panic tore through the hospital.",
        "visual_keywords": "medical lab syringe examination"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Twenty-three staff members dropped with mysterious symptoms, forcing a total evacuation of the ER into the parking lot. Was this a biohazard leak, or was her body producing a deadly poison?",
        "visual_keywords": "evacuation hospital night panic"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Scientists at Lawrence Livermore Laboratory finally cracked the puzzle. Ramirez had been applying a common pain relief cream called DMSO, which accumulated in her failing kidneys.",
        "visual_keywords": "scientific laboratory chemical testing"
      },
      {
        "narration": "When paramedics pumped high-pressure oxygen into her system, it chemically transformed that harmless cream into dimethyl sulfate\u2014a lethal chemical weapon inside her own veins.",
        "visual_keywords": "glowing chemical molecular structure"
      }
    ],
